(backend: real MongoDB at mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017: 7.0.45)
loaded 1140 match documents and 800 raw payloads
one match document is about 5.1 KB of JSON (lineups + shots embedded)

--- M1. point read: the whole match page in ONE document read
    Northgate United 2-1 Marlowe United   home line-up size=11   (PostgreSQL needed joins across 5 tables for this)

--- M2. team's fixtures in one season, newest first (uses the compound index)
    MD38: Oakhurst Rovers 0-1 Northgate United
    MD37: Northgate United 1-1 Glenmoor United
    MD36: Dunmore Rovers 1-1 Northgate United

--- M3. aggregation pipeline: top 5 scorers of 2025/26 (unwind line-ups)
    Ade Santos             12
    Chidi Silva            11
    Omar Costa             10
    Femi Silva             10
    Ezra Adeyemi           10

--- M4. array query ($elemMatch): matches with a stoppage-time goal (after minute 90)
    114 matches

--- M5. schema-on-read: four providers, four shapes, one collection
    provider_a: top-level payload keys = ['events', 'matchId']
    provider_b: top-level payload keys = ['general', 'stats']
    provider_c: top-level payload keys = ['shotmap']
    provider_d: top-level payload keys = ['lineups', 'schema_version']
    provider_d payloads already on schema_version 2: 100 (versions can be mixed - no migration needed to ingest)

--- trade-off check: updating a goal in a document vs PostgreSQL
    one corrected score touches: match_documents.home.goals, away.goals AND the embedded lineup.goals AND the shots list
    -> the same fact lives in several places; the application (or a change stream) must keep them in step.
    In PostgreSQL the same correction is one row in `match` (plus the derived standings, Portfolio 3).
